"executive_summary": "This Feature Article reviews recent work on bioinspired supramolecular materials that mimic the dynamic contact events occurring at biological interfaces between and within cells. The authors argue that cellular activities such as adhesion, signal transduction, and substance transportation are driven by dynamic biomacromolecules and supramolecular assemblies including extracellular matrices, phospholipid bilayers, and membrane proteins, and that mimicking these dynamic actions is an effective route to biomaterials for regenerative medicine and drug delivery. The article highlights two material classes: artificial extracellular matrices built from self-assembling peptide hydrogels, and membrane-deforming supramolecular materials. For the ECM class, the authors describe stiffness-controlled supramolecular peptide hydrogels based on RADA16 and its glycine mutants A16G and A8G, showing that fibre morphology tunes hydrogel stiffness and that A16G-N-cadherin conjugates markedly enhance neuroblast migration in an injured neonatal neocortex in vivo. They also describe JigSAP fibre-forming peptides with jigsaw-shaped hydrophobic surfaces that efficiently incorporate and sustain-release full-length proteins such as VEGF, promoting angiogenesis in an ischaemic stroke mouse model. For kinetics control, redox-responsive peptides bearing methionine residues undergo gel-to-sol transitions upon oxidation by hydrogen peroxide, enabling stimulus-controlled cargo release. For the membrane class, the authors describe highly stable phospholipid nanosheets (bicelles) stabilized by a cholic acid–metal-binding-peptide surfactant crosslinked with copper ions, which show prolonged blood circulation relative to spherical vesicles. Finally, they describe an amphiphilic molecular machine (AzoMEx) that undergoes light-triggered opening/closing motion to expand a giant unilamellar vesicle membrane and induce endocytosis-like fission, enabling efficient encapsulation of micrometer-size M13 phage viruses and their delivery to multiple organs via blood administration. The authors conclude that these supramolecular approaches to mimicking and controlling bio-interfaces present powerful ways to develop regenerative medicines and drug delivery systems, while noting that the materials science of reconstructing biomimetic dynamic interfaces is still in its infancy. The article is a review rather than an empirical study, so it reports no participant sample size, no numerical sample, and no statistical metrics; accordingly, fields that require reported data or code are set to empty strings. The narrative is grounded in verbatim fragments from the text, including specific incorporation percentages, release percentages, stiffness values, dilution conditions, and organ infectivity values, which are preserved with all key numbers.",
